## 3. How each heuristic enters (formulas and predicted sign)

**Predictor scale (Mechanism 1; Eq. 9; Prop. J.2).**
- `W_p → αW_p` gives `p_ℓ ∝ α²` and `q_ℓ ∝ α`, so `µ_kℓ(α) = µ_kℓ(1)/α`.
- "nothing escapes above `α_ext = max_kℓ µ_kℓ(1)`".
- Predicted sign: smaller init ⇒ larger µ ⇒ escape ("one must initialize the
  predictor with a sufficiently small variance").
- Fig. 3: measured µ follows `1/α` for `p_{m1} ≤ 0.4`, and bends downward for
  `p_{m1} ≥ 0.5`.
- **Not treated:** predictor width, depth, and learning rate get no formula.
  Depth appears only as an experimental factor (ImageNet-100 depth 2/6;
  App. K depth 2/6/12). The predictor learning-rate ratio `η_p` appears only in
  Thm F.3 (drift ∝ `η_p`), in App. F.6.3 (104/104, 104/104 and 102/104
  sign-classification at 1×, 3× and 10× the predictor rate), and in an App. I.8
  BYOL check where rank and accuracy increase with the predictor/encoder lr
  ratio over {0, 0.1, 1, 10} (2 seeds).

**Masking ratio (Mechanism 2; Eq. 10; Eq. 38).**
- `Σ_X1 ≈ p_{m1} Σ_XX`. Diagonal entries of `Σ_11` scale as `p_{m1}`,
  off-diagonals as `p_{m1}²`.
- `µ_i` therefore "depends on how the data covariance is structured".
- Stated sign: "Low context ratios, corresponding to higher masking, reduce the
  correlation between the context and target views, weakening ... γ and
  subsequently lowering µ" (§5).
- For a unit-diagonal `Σ_XX` with spectrum `{s_k}` (Prop. J.6):
  `a_k = p(1−p) + p² s_k` and `c_k = p s_k` [OUR ALGEBRA, consistent with
  J.6]. So `c_k/a_k = s_k/((1−p) + p s_k)`.
- **[UNCLEAR]** The paper calls `p_{m1}` both the "context masking ratio"
  (Fig. 4, Mechanism 2 title) and the "keep probability" (§5). Throughout it
  behaves as a keep probability (higher = less masking).
- The target mask enters only as the scalar `p_tgt` inside
  `q_ℓ = eig(W_pᵀM̄_2)`.

**EMA (Mechanism 3; Prop. J.7).** "β multiplies the constant term without
changing its sign, so for a real drive spectrum it cannot decide whether a mode
escapes; it acts as a timescale separator: for µ_i ≪ 1, smaller β slows
collapse, while for µ_i ≫ 1, it throttles feature growth."
- The escape rate `λ⁺(β) = ½[−(β+σ) + √((β−σ)² + 4βγ)]` is strictly increasing
  in β.
- As β → ∞ (proportional target), `λ⁺ → γ − σ` (Cor. A.4(4)).
- For a complex drive, β *does* enter the escape condition (Eq. 30).
- Tab. 25 check: β = 0.01 to 50, escape count 8 at all five values.

**Weight decay (Prop. J.1; Tab. 3).** Encoder weight decay only: `σ′_kℓ =
σ_kℓ + η_wd`, and a mode escapes iff `γ_kℓ − σ_kℓ > η_wd`, equivalently
`µ_i > 1 + η_wd/σ_i`. Tab. 25: five doses × 3 seeds, predicted counts
24/19/8/2/0, all matched. App. J.2 also reproduces Tian 2021's DirectPred
weight-decay "death threshold" `η > τ² max_k(c_k² p_tgt²/a_k)/4`, as the
supremum over predictor eigenvalue `p̂` of `τ c_k p_tgt p̂/(a_k p̂² + η)`.

**Stop-gradient.** It is built into Eq. 1 and never varied or analysed. There
is no derivation of the no-stop-gradient case. The Conclusion still says the
framing "clarifies the role of EMA, stop-gradient, and predictor design".
**[UNCLEAR: no supporting derivation found.]**

**Other mechanisms (Tab. 3/25, App. J).**
- SIGReg: `p_ℓ → p_ℓ − ρ`, escape iff `µ > 1 − ρ/p_ℓ` (Prop. J.3).
- VICReg hinge: `p_ℓ − λ̃` (Prop. J.4).
- Mimetic init: a decaying dose (§6 below).
- Frozen identity predictor (Prop. J.6): `P = I`, `Q = p_tgt I`,
  `µ_k = p_tgt s_k/((1 − p) + p s_k)`. A mode escapes iff
  `s_k > (1 − p)/(p_tgt − p)`, which "requires p_tgt > p". "Under symmetric
  additive view noise instead of masking (the BYOL-style setting), c_k/a_k < 1
  for every mode and nothing escapes."
- Also recovered as limits (App. J.2): DirectPred, Halvagal et al. 2023's rate
  equation and IsoLoss, and Richemond et al. 2023's orthonormal predictor.

